WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.568
-And I'm so excited
to have our first guest.

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.604
He is a best-selling author
and the Emmy-winning host

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.872
of "All In with Chris Hayes"

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.208
which airs weeknights at 8:00
on MSNBC.

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:11.110
He also hosts the podcast
"Why Is This Happening?"

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:14.147
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend, Chris Hayes.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.649
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:26.659 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:31.497 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:33.132
-How are you?
-I'm great!

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:34.767
This is great!
-Isn't it nice?

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.702
-It really is.
-We should note,

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:38.337
you've been on the show a lot,
but we looked back,

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:41.841
and the last time you were here
was the day of the inauguration.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.410
And you had to sit
on the other side of the couch.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.611
There was nobody here.
-That's right.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:47.947
At that point,
COVID was only traveling 5 feet.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.316
-Yeah.
So COVID would go,

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.518
but it would just lose steam
and like -- yeah.

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:54.654
-Right there. It's over there.
-So what would happen is,

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:56.722
in between, the crew would come
and clean up all the COVID

00:00:56.722 --> 00:00:57.790
that had landed
between the desk.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.859
-Exactly.
-So, we talked kind of --

00:01:00.859 --> 00:01:02.829
inauguration about
how it felt like

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.530
we had just avoided a car crash.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.966
Ad so, it wasn't a situation
where you felt joy,

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:10.002
it was more like
adrenaline and excitement.

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:11.537
Now we're nine months in.

00:01:11.537 --> 00:01:13.772
It also does not feel
particularly joyous right now.

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:15.941
-Yeah, well, I think
there was a real --

00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:17.710
For me, there was
a kind of a window of joy,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.580
which was that post --
when I got the vaccine in April.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.214
-Yeah.

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:23.682
-We hadn't been hit
by delta yet.

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:26.018
My brother got married.
He had delayed this wedding.

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:28.320
An actual wedding,
it felt incredibly joyful.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.622
And then I think --

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.424
A huge part of it
was just delta coming back.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.893
-Yeah.
-And watching this play out,

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:37.830
and the really needless human
suffering that came with that.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.666
And then, I think, also,
this feeling of, like,

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:43.302
you know when you're
troubleshooting something,

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:45.304
and you unplug it
and plug it back in,

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:47.239
and you're like,
"Please, just work."

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:49.241
And it's like, "Oh, the error
message is still there."

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:51.310
-Yeah.
-And it feels like that

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.145
a little bit with, like,
American society.

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:54.847
-Yeah.
-It's like, "Oh, right.

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:56.615
The error message
is still there."

00:01:56.615 --> 00:02:00.053
Delta's still there,
the deep divisions,

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:04.424
the incipient and blossoming
authoritarian anti-Democratic

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:07.259
movement capturing
one of our two parties,

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:11.764
the anti-vax disinformation,
all of that is still there.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.532
And I think just
dealing with that,

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:14.900
and dealing with the fact
that we're living through

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.636
this period of extended
abnormality is really hard.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:20.038
-It also -- you know,
the results of the election,

00:02:20.038 --> 00:02:22.508
obviously, was something,
I think, that gave some of us

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:23.809
a great amount of hope.

00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:26.278
And then the result for
the people who were upset,

00:02:26.278 --> 00:02:29.015
it seemed like they've been
very coldly efficient

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:33.453
in using that loss to sort of
gird for this next election.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:39.158
And is it as troubling
as I think I might worry

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.660
that it's going to be?

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.596
-I'm pretty worried about it.
I mean, I think --

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:46.465
One thing to think about here
is that --

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:49.969
One thing I think we've learned
is that the way democracies work

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:53.139
is through a kind
of social consensus

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:55.775
about the winner winning
and the loser losing.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.143
And when you take that away,

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.245
you're faced with something
much more perilous.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.114
And then when you take that way
and then you're faced with

00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:03.916
someone that
wants to essentially

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:08.253
create a litmus test,
a defining issue around

00:03:08.253 --> 00:03:10.523
this "stabbed in the back"
narrative, you know,

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.492
you're dealing with stuff
that's, like, really, really

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:15.594
dangerous to American democracy.
And I think --

00:03:15.594 --> 00:03:17.463
You know, human beings
are really good at adapting.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.565
I think I said this before on
your show in the midst of COVID.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:20.833
But it's, like, you know,
we're just like,

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:22.669
"Oh, well, January 6th happened,
and, oh, yeah,

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:24.270
once-in-a-century pandemic.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.672
You know, "What can I watch
on Netflix tonight?"

00:03:26.672 --> 00:03:28.174
It's like --
-Yeah.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:32.011
-But that stuff is real, and
it's urgent, and it's present.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.213
And I think it's hard
to sort of see it for what it is

00:03:34.213 --> 00:03:35.548
just day by day.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.216
-You know, one of the things
that Democrats have this

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:39.585
very small window to do,
it strikes me, before midterms,

00:03:39.585 --> 00:03:42.888
is show people that they're
a party worth governing.

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:44.423
-Yes.
-And it strikes me that

00:03:44.423 --> 00:03:47.026
this bill right now,
the Build Back Better bill --

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:49.562
Bad name, right?
-Yeah. Not great.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:53.399
-No.
I feel like we correctly said

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:56.636
"Be best" was a terrible name
for Melania's --

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:58.838
-Nothing beats "Be best."
"Be best better."

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:01.040
-No, but Build Back Better
is, like,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.842
only a little bit better
than "Be best."

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:06.479
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.080
-But, you know,
we've talk about it.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.983
We had Senator Elizabeth Warren
here yesterday.

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:13.953
And a lot of people have framed
this bill as a number, right?

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.187
$3.5 trillion.
-Yeah.

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:16.456
I think that's a thing
that people on the street

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.957
could tell you far faster
than they could tell you

00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:20.459
what is in this bill.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.429
Have Democrats done a bad job
telling this story?

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:25.498
Has the media done a bad job
reporting this story?

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:26.799
-I think it's three things.
I think it's --

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.601
the media has done a pretty
bad job of telling it.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.903
I think Democrats have not done
a great job, as well.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.872
But the weird thing, though, is,
this is all reverse engineered

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:36.108
around the procedural
imperative.

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:38.945
They get one
reconciliation bill -- right? --

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.114
To avoid the 60-vote threshold
because they don't want

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:43.149
to get rid of the filibuster,
at least Manchin and Sinema

00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:45.050
and maybe a few others don't.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.786
So that means everything's
got to go in the one bill.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:48.388
So then it's very hard to
message a bill that's got

00:04:48.388 --> 00:04:50.322
six or seven
different things in it,

00:04:50.322 --> 00:04:51.891
which,
under normal circumstances,

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:54.093
you could pass, like,
"Here's our elder-care bill

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.294
and our child-care bill

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.229
and our climate
and utility bill

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:01.700
and our carbon-free bill,
and our paid-family-leave bill.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.169
All that stuff is good stuff
that would be popular.

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:06.205
Instead, it's all
put into a blender,

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.941
and you've got the, like,
Build Back Better smoothie.

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:10.676
[ Light laughter ]

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.711
And it's a little hard, I think,
to sell that

00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:15.147
partly because of this weird
procedural constraint.

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.417
The other thing I'll say about
the price, it drives me insane.

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:20.319
-Yeah.
-If I said to you,

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:24.023
"Okay. I have one thing here
that's $27 and this is $65."

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:27.793
It's like, which do you want?
It's like, "I don't know."

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:31.063
"Oh, well, this is a bunch
of rat carcasses for $27."

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:33.332
"Well, I don't want that."
And like, "This is actually

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.567
an apartment in New York City,
two bedrooms, 65 bucks."

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:36.769
Like, "Yes, please,
give that to me!"

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:39.305
-Yeah.
-The price means nothing

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.240
without an awareness
of what's in it.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.310
And I agree that that has really
come to define what the bill is.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.245
-And, obviously now, you know,
I think there's a frustration

00:05:47.245 --> 00:05:50.616
for people who would say,
"Hey, you guys are in charge.

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.218
You have the House.
You have the Senate.

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:54.219
You have the presidency.
Democrats, what's your problem?"

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:57.690
But the reality is,
the Democrats have the Senate

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.826
not only by a razor-thin margin,
but it's a kind of a miracle

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:02.628
they have
50 Democratic senators.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:05.865
-I mean, we -- I think for that
period between Election Day

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:07.099
and January 5th,
which was the day

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:08.767
that those two Georgia
senators were elected,

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:10.669
I think the expectation was,
it was gonna be

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:12.405
McConnell's Senate,
nothing was going to get done.

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:16.008
It was maybe not being able
to confirm any nominees.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.676
The fact they have
the majority is a miracle.

00:06:17.676 --> 00:06:19.245
The fact they passed
the American Rescue Plan

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:20.479
as quickly as they did
is sort of a miracle.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:23.382
So when you take a step back,
you think,

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:25.084
"Where do you think you would be
in December

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:27.586
before those
two improbable victories?"

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:29.121
and we're in better shape.
The other thing I always

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:33.259
say to people is, "Look,
you're asking full consensus."

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:36.395
Like, if I took 50 people from
this audience, and were like,

00:06:36.395 --> 00:06:38.764
"What movie
should we watch tonight?"

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:41.300
you could not get them to agree.
-Yeah.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.270
-When you're 15 family members,
you're like,

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.471
"What should we eat for dinner?"

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:47.706
-I will say,
I could get 50 of them to agree

00:06:47.706 --> 00:06:49.508
"The Old Scottish Detective"
was a dud.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:50.843
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:50.843 --> 00:06:53.546
I could find --
maybe even 100.

00:06:53.546 --> 00:06:55.748
We'll be right back
with more from Chris Hayes.

